<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clermont-Ferrand</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Augustonemetum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Clermont-Ferrand is a city in the Auvergne in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, at the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limagne">Limagne</a> plain beneath the volcanic Chaîne des Puys. It began as Augustonemetum, the Roman capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a>, and as Clermont it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a> preached the First Crusade in 1095. It later merged with neighbouring Montferrand and is the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelin">Michelin</a> tyre company.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clermont-ferrand.jpg" alt="Clermont-Ferrand" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fabien1309 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ville_de_Clermont-Ferrand.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Clermont-Ferrand lies on the plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limagne">Limagne</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massif-central">Massif Central</a>, surrounded by the chain of volcanoes called the Chaîne des Puys, which includes the dormant Puy de Dôme, 10 kilometres away. The mountains put the city in a rain shadow, so it has one of the driest climates in metropolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast, with cold winters and hot summers. The old part of Clermont follows the line of the ramparts as they stood at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and the city's cathedral is built of black lava stone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first known mention is by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, who called it the metropolis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a>, then named Nemessos, a Gaulish word for a sacred forest, on the mound where the cathedral now stands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> was born somewhere near it around 72 BCE and repulsed the Roman assault at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gergovia">Gergovia</a> in 52 BCE. After the Roman conquest it was Augustonemetum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> for sanctuary of Augustus. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat in the 5th century under Namatius, and the emperor Julius Nepos ceded it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> in 475. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> took it a generation after the Visigoths lost it in 507. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> ravaged it in 862 and 864 and again around 898.</p><p>The castle of Clarus Mons gave its name to the town in 848. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a> called the First Crusade at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-clermont">Council of Clermont</a> in 1095. In 1120 the counts of Auvergne founded the rival town of Montferrand next to Clermont's walls to counter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' power. Clermont became a royal town in 1551, and the Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a> of 1630 joined the two towns, confirmed in 1731 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a>. Montferrand demanded independence three times, in 1789, 1848 and 1863.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelin">Michelin</a> factories and the city gardens built in the 20th century united the two towns, though two distinct downtowns survive and Montferrand keeps a strong identity. Clermont-Ferrand is the prefecture of Puy-de-Dôme and had a population of 146,351 in 2023. It is the headquarters of Michelin and hosts an international short film festival. Its Romanesque church of Notre-Dame du Port was nominated as a World Heritage Site in 1998, and the cathedral, in Gothic style, was built between the 13th and 19th centuries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Clermont-Ferrand” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Jerusalem was created by the First Crusade, launched after Pope Urban II’s appeal at <b>Clermont</b> in 1095.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Geoffrey I built the great castle of Chlemoutsi, or <b>Clermont</b>, in about 1220–1223, reputedly with church revenues he had seized, and strongholds such as Karytaina, Akova and Kalavryta guarded the baronies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Arverni were treated leniently, and their Roman capital <b>Augustonemetum</b> grew into Clermont-Ferrand.</blockquote><a href="/state/arverni">Arverni</a></li><li><blockquote>In the later 10th century the viscounts of <b>Clermont</b> took the comital title, and by around 980 they governed the county as hereditary counts under the loose overlordship of the dukes of Aquitaine.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-auvergne">County of Auvergne</a></li></ul></section>
